# `src/llm` — Reorganization for Multi-Protocol Support

**Status:** proposal (not yet implemented)
**Goal:** re-organize this module so the OpenAI **Responses** API can be added alongside the existing **Chat Completions** API, reusing everything that is protocol-agnostic and isolating everything that isn't.

---

## 1. Executive summary

Today the module has one protocol (Chat Completions) and one concrete `Chat`
implementation, and the two are braided together: the concrete class mixes
session management, the tool-execution loop, request encoding, and SSE
decoding, and several protocol details (raw tool-call JSON, `choices[].delta`
streaming, `prompt_tokens` usage fields) leak across the module boundary into
the app layer.

The reorganization splits the module into three clean layers:

| Layer | Contents | Protocol-aware? |
|---|---|---|
| **Core** (`LLM`) | `Chat` (config, tool registry, tool loop, session management), `Connection` (transport), `Message`/`Content` (canonical model), `History` (turns, compaction, branch, save/restore), `Function`/`LocalFunction`, `FunctionCall`, `Usage`, `ChatInclusions`, `ResponseSchema`, event vocabulary | No |
| **Protocol adapters** (`LLM::Protocol::*`) | one module per wire protocol: request encoder, response/stream decoder, streaming tool-call accumulator, and a concrete `Chat` subclass implementing the one abstract method `post_session` | Yes |
| **Providers** (`LLM::OpenAI`, `LLM::Ollama`, …) | endpoint, auth, headers; each declares the single protocol its backend speaks (primary = highest fidelity) and a `new_chat` factory | No (they *declare* a protocol) |

| Reuse as-is | Decouple (move to core, strip wire format) | New |
|---|---|---|
| `Function` / `LocalFunction` / `Function::Reply` (tools, side-effects, attachments) | `Message` hierarchy + `Content` parts (drop `to_protocol_json`; encoders serialize instead) | `Protocol::Responses` adapter (encoder, decoder, chat) |
| `Connection` transport (HTTP client, retry, session header) | `History` (move out of `LLM::OpenAI`; keep logic identical) | `LLM::FunctionCall` canonical value object |
| Session logic: squish, branch/fork, tail, extract, `exclude_past_reasoning`, MCP `import` | `OpenAI::Chat` (split into core methods + thin protocol `Chat`) | `LLM::ProtocolKind` enum, `LLM::Events` constants |
| `ChatInclusions`, `ResponseSchema`, `Conversation`/`Reasoning` enums, `ChatEvent` alias | `Usage` (move to core; per-protocol field mapping) | Provider protocol declaration + `new_chat` wiring + CC compatibility override |
| Tool-execution loop (`call_tools_and_setup_ask`) — lifted into base `Chat` | CC streaming parser (stays, but confined to the CC adapter) | Golden-file tests for encoder/decoder |

Emergent benefit: because history becomes a single **canonical** model,
sessions are portable — `fork`, save/restore, and conversation transfer work
across protocols, and a user could switch a running session's protocol.

